---
import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import CommandBlock from "../components/data/CommandBlock.astro";
import EvidenceLinks from "../components/data/EvidenceLinks.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { withBase } from "../lib/urls";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Help the next branch grow."
  eyebrow="Open source / Code, words and evidence"
  description="Useful contributions begin with a real problem, a reproducible observation, or a clearer explanation. There is room for more than code."
>
  <div class="content-split">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Report what you can reproduce.</h2>
      <p>
        Search <a href={`${site.github}/issues`}>existing issues</a>, then
        describe the problem, expected behavior and exact reproduction steps.
        Include Radivine version, OS, desktop/window manager, X11/Wayland
        session, file manager, architecture and scale. Use disposable sample
        files and redact diagnostics.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={`${site.github}/issues/new`}>Open a non-security issue</a>. A
        useful compatibility report tests Drag + Shift, Invoke, Properties,
        clipboard and startup separately. Missing evidence is not proof of
        incompatibility.
      </p>
      <h2>Code and desktop testing.</h2>
      <p>
        Read the <a href={site.contribute}>contribution guidelines</a> and{" "}
        <a href={`${site.source}/docs/TESTING_STRATEGY.md`}>testing strategy</a>
        . Keep changes focused and use Conventional Commits. Preserve protected
        branding and the platform-independent wheel/renderer boundaries.
      </p>
      <p>
        The release CI reference is Rust 1.95.0; the older guide’s lower
        compiler minimum has not been independently established. Use an isolated
        Xvfb display for GUI automation, never your active desktop.
      </p>
      <CommandBlock
        title="Headless Rust checks"
        code={
          'DISPLAY="" cargo fmt --all --check\nDISPLAY="" cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\nDISPLAY="" cargo test --workspace'
        }
      />
      <p>
        Headless success alone does not certify a live compositor, file manager,
        or screen reader. Check the contribution guide before running isolated
        X11 tests.
      </p>
    </article>
    <aside class="prose">
      <h2>Words and website work.</h2>
      <p>
        Improve installation steps, explain a limitation, or supply a dated
        compatibility report. Product claims need pinned source, tests, a
        recorded environment, or a published release.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a checkout containing this website, use the pinned Node 24.22.1
        environment:
      </p>
      <CommandBlock
        title="Website checks"
        code={
          "cd website\nnpm ci\nnpm run check\nnpm run test:content\nnpm run format:check\nnpm run build\nnpm run check:links\nnpm test"
        }
      />
      <p>
        Browser tests need Playwright Chromium and its OS libraries. Keep
        website work separate from Rust release workflows. Read the repository’s
        AGENTS.md before agent-assisted contributions.
      </p>
      <h2>Real product media.</h2>
      <p>
        Current Mint desktop captures are welcome: record version, date,
        session, monitor scale, configuration, sample files and whether the
        image is live UI or a renderer export. Remove personal material by
        preparing the scene; never relabel an old screenshot.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
  <section class="prose content-section">
    <h2>Security reports belong in private.</h2>
    <p>
      Use{" "}
      <a href={`${site.github}/security/advisories/new`}>
        GitHub private vulnerability reporting
      </a>
      , or contact{" "}
      <a href="mailto:marawan6569@gmail.com?subject=%5BSECURITY%5D%20Radivine">
        the maintainer by email
      </a>{" "}
      with the subject “[SECURITY] Radivine”. Include affected
      component/version, reproduction and impact. Do not post private inputs or
      exploit details in a public issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      The repository security policy’s supported-version table is historical.
      This page makes no support-lifetime promise for the current release.
    </p>
    <EvidenceLinks ids={["contributing", "security"]} />
  </section>
  <nav class="page-links" aria-label="Community resources">
    <a href={`${site.source}/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`}>Code of conduct ↗</a>
    <a href={withBase("licenses/")}>Licenses and notices →</a>
    <a href={withBase("roadmap/")}>Project direction →</a>
  </nav>
</ContentLayout>
